msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:50
msgid "// The loop can borrow the grid without taking ownership.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:52
msgid "\"point = {x}, {y}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:55
msgid "// The grid remains accessible after the loop.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:62
msgid ""
"By implementing `IntoIterator` on `&Grid`, we can pass a reference into a "
"`for` loop and get a borrowing iterator. This allows us to borrow the grid "
"object in the loop without taking ownership of it."
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:66
msgid ""
"[This playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?"
"version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=c3c29974bac8cc2065ca1b6608d5c8b9) "
"shows that we can have both `GridIter` and `GridRefIter` at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:69
msgid ""
"This pattern is used heavily in the standard library for its collection "
"types, allowing for owning and borrowing iterators to be created implicitly "
"based on whether you pass the object in by value, by reference, or by "
"mutable reference."
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:74
msgid ""
"Pull up the [`IntoIterator`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait."
"IntoIterator.html) docs and show the implementations for `[T; N]`, `&[T; "
"N]`, and `&mut [T; N]`. Show that they produce different iterators with "
"different item types:"
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:78
msgid "The iterator for `[T; N]` produces `T` by value."
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:79
msgid "The iterator for `&[T; N]` produces `&T`."
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/intoiterator-refs.md:80
msgid "The iterator for `&mut [T; N]` produces `&mut T`."
msgstr ""
